# net-failover -- WiFi networks to fall back to, in PRIORITY ORDER.
# The first entry that is in range AND actually reaches the internet wins.
#
# Copy to C:\ProgramData\net-failover\networks.conf (install.ps1 does this for
# you on a first install) and fill in your own networks. This file contains NO
# real credentials and the live copy is never committed -- it is readable by
# Administrators and SYSTEM only.
#
# Save the live copy as UTF-8 (Notepad's default) if any SSID has non-ASCII
# characters -- the daemon reads it as UTF-8.
#
# Format:   SSID|PASSWORD|FLAGS
#
#   SSID       the network name, exactly as broadcast (case sensitive)
#   PASSWORD   WPA/WPA2 passphrase, plain text.
#              Use @saved to reuse a profile already stored in Windows.
#              Leave empty for an open network.
#   FLAGS      optional, comma separated. Currently supported: hidden
#
# See what is in range (elevated):   net-failover -Scan
# Apply changes (elevated):
#   Stop-ScheduledTask -TaskName net-failover; Start-ScheduledTask -TaskName net-failover

# --- examples, replace with your own -----------------------------------------

# HomeRouter|correct-horse-battery-staple
# Office-5G|another-passphrase
# BackupAP|third-passphrase|hidden
# OpenCafeWiFi|

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ordering advice
#
# WiFi served by the SAME router that feeds your ethernet port is a good
# fallback for a dead cable, switch or NIC -- but not for an ISP outage, since
# that router's WiFi is equally offline in that case.
#
# A phone / 4G hotspot is the only entry that survives an ISP outage, so it is
# worth listing as a last resort. To confirm an uplink really is independent,
# compare public IPs (get each adapter's IPv4 from ipconfig or net-failover
# -Status, then bind a probe to it):
#
#   curl.exe --interface <ethernet-ip> https://api.ipify.org
#   curl.exe --interface <wifi-ip>     https://api.ipify.org
#
# Different answers mean genuinely separate uplinks -- even if both hand out
# addresses on the same private subnet.
#
# A stored WPA key is hashed together with the SSID, so a key saved under a
# misspelled SSID can never authenticate against the correct one. If a network
# refuses to associate, re-enter the plain-text passphrase here rather than
# relying on @saved.
